# The deployment's configuration (P16-01, P17-01).
#
# Copy to `~/ds-education/config.env` **on the server**, fill it in once, and
# leave it there. Deployments never overwrite it, and nothing in it is in git.
#
#   install -m 600 /dev/null ~/ds-education/config.env
#   $EDITOR ~/ds-education/config.env
#
# ## What is *not* here
#
# The database passwords and the encryption key. `deploy.sh` generates those on
# first run into `~/ds-education/secrets.env` and never regenerates them —
# nobody reads them, nobody types them, and no decision is encoded in 32 random
# bytes. See `secrets.sh`.
#
# The four service hostnames, the API origin, the staff cookie's scope, the CORS
# allow-list, the portal's base URL and the widget's URL. All derived from
# BASE_DOMAIN by `domains.sh`. You may set any of them explicitly and it wins;
# you should not need to.
#
# ## What is here
#
# Answers. Things no default can be right about.

# --- The domain -------------------------------------------------------------
# A bare domain: no scheme, no port, no path, no trailing dot. Its A/AAAA
# records must resolve to this host before the first deploy — Caddy proves
# control of each hostname with an HTTP-01 challenge on :80.
BASE_DOMAIN=digitalspital.com


# Where Let's Encrypt sends expiry warnings. A real, monitored mailbox — this
# is the last warning before a certificate lapses.
ACME_EMAIL=technik@digitalspital.de

# ===========================================================================
# Per-surface settings
# ===========================================================================
# Everything below names a *customer*, and is here only because the surface it
# configures is a browser app that must know these things before it has spoken
# to any API. Nothing above this line does.
#
# The direction of travel is that all of it moves onto the project row, where
# the rest of a customer's configuration already lives — see P18-03 and P18-04.
# Until then, a second customer means a second portal hostname and a second
# block here, which is the honest cost of it still being in a file.

# --- The portal ------------------------------------------------------------
# `fortbildung.<BASE_DOMAIN>` shows one project's catalogue, so a project is
# what that hostname *is*. Names a row in `projects`.

# The portal's own OIDC client — a learner-facing *public* client with the
# portal's redirect URI, separate from anything the customer's WordPress site
# uses so the two revoke independently.
#
# **Nothing here names a customer's identity provider any more (P18-04).**
# `PORTAL_KEYCLOAK_ISSUER` and `PORTAL_KEYCLOAK_CLIENT_ID` used to live here and
# are gone: the portal has not run an OIDC flow since P21-03 — a federated
# tenant gets a link to the customer's own login — and which realm a project
# validates against is `projects.keycloak_issuer`, per request, which is what
# lets one installation serve several customers with separate realms.

# --- The admin console -----------------------------------------------------
# A *default*, not an identity. A super administrator spans customers and their
# first screen is the customer registry, which sends no project header at all;
# this is what the tenant-scoped screens use until the operator picks one.
# P18-03 makes that a choice in the interface and removes this line.

# --- Origins allowed to call the API ---------------------------------------
# The console and the portal are added automatically from BASE_DOMAIN, and that
# is the whole of it.
#
# `EXTRA_CORS_ORIGINS` used to be here — the list of customers' own sites that
# may embed the widget — and it is now **`projects.embed_origins`**, set per
# project in the admin console (P18-04). It had to move: one list in one env
# file is the union across every customer on the installation, so adding a
# second customer widened the permission for the first.
#
# The obstacle that kept it here for three tickets was that a CORS preflight
# carries no `X-DS-Project` header, so the API cannot know which project is
# being asked about. It does not need to: CORS asks whether an *origin* may
# talk to this API at all, and that is answerable by looking the origin up
# across every project. The tenant boundary is enforced where it always was —
# the request's `X-DS-Project`, the guard, and RLS.

# --- The bare domain (optional) ---------------------------------------------
# `digitalspital.com` itself is not one of the four service hostnames — it is
# free for a promotional site. Until there is one, setting this makes Caddy
# serve a redirect there so the bare domain is not a TLS error; leave it empty
# and Caddy has no site block for the apex at all.
# What the bare domain does. Defaults to the portal, so somebody typing
# digitalspital.com lands on the Fortbildungen.
#
# It has a default because the alternative turned out to be a browser security
# error: the DNS wildcard points the apex here, so "no site block" means Caddy
# has no certificate for the name and the TLS handshake fails outright.
#
# Set it to a marketing site when there is one, or to `none` to serve nothing
# from the apex — and if you choose `none`, point its DNS away from this host
# first.
APEX_REDIRECT_URL=

# --- Alerting ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Where a Punktemeldung deadline alarm is posted. Optional: every alert is
# logged at `error` regardless, so log-based alerting is the floor. A webhook is
# what turns that into something that reaches a person on a Saturday — which
# matters, because the Ärztekammer's reporting window is 8 days and its paper
# fallback closes with it.
#
# Generic JSON, so a Slack/Teams incoming webhook, PagerDuty, or an internal
# endpoint all work. The payload carries an enrolment id and an urgency, never
# an EFN or a name.
ALERT_WEBHOOK_URL=

# --- Database name ----------------------------------------------------------
# The passwords are generated; only the names are decisions.
POSTGRES_DB=ds_education
POSTGRES_SUPERUSER=postgres

# --- Body size limits -------------------------------------------------------
# The font upload needs its own: the column allows a 2 MB file and base64
# inflates that to ~2.8 MB. Everything else stays at 1 MB — a JSON parser is a
# denial-of-service surface.
MAX_REQUEST_BODY_SIZE=1mb
MAX_FONT_BODY_SIZE=3mb

# --- Certificate delivery (P8-03) ------------------------------------------
# SMTP is configured per project in the admin console, not here. These are the
# worker's knobs. Delivery never gates the download: a certificate that cannot
# be emailed is still downloadable, and shows as "unzustellbar" in the
# participant list. The download link's base is derived (PORTAL_BASE_URL).
CERTIFICATE_DELIVERY_ENABLED=yes
CERTIFICATE_DELIVERY_INTERVAL_SEC=300
CERTIFICATE_DELIVERY_BATCH_SIZE=25

# --- EIV-FOBI --------------------------------------------------------------
# Leave pointing at the mock until the ÄKWL questions in docs/show-stoppers.md
# are answered. A Punktemeldung cannot be withdrawn once the 7-day correction
# window closes, so the live endpoint additionally requires EIV_ALLOW_LIVE=yes
# — and the deploy script refuses the combination of a live URL without it.
#
# The real endpoint, when the time comes: https://punktemeldung.eiv-fobi.de/
EIV_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4010
EIV_ALLOW_LIVE=
# Exactly one submitter, however many API containers run. Two workers racing
# for the same submission is a duplicate report to the Ärztekammer.
EIV_WORKER_ENABLED=yes

# How much reaches the log (P25-01). One JSON object per line, always
# redacted. `info` is one line per request plus every warning and error;
# `debug` adds detail nobody wants by default and everybody wants during an
# incident. Changing it is a container restart, not a deploy.
LOG_LEVEL=info

# --- Object storage (optional) ---------------------------------------------
# All of it or none of it. Empty means courses serve media from plain https://
# URLs; set means a course row may hold `s3://<customerId>/courses/<id>/<file>`
# and the API signs it per object, briefly, after the gate agrees.
#
# S3-compatible, in Germany. Hetzner Object Storage endpoints look like
# https://fsn1.your-objectstorage.com (region fsn1 / eu-central-1).
S3_ENDPOINT=
S3_REGION=
S3_BUCKET=
S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE=yes
S3_URL_TTL_SEC=3600
# How long an *upload* signature stays usable. Bounds when the PUT may start,
# not how long it may take: S3 checks the expiry as the request arrives, so a
# large file that began in time keeps going.
S3_UPLOAD_TTL_SEC=1800

# --- Bucket configuration you have to do once, by hand ---------------------
#
# Two settings live at the bucket and cannot be set from here.
#
# **1. CORS.** The browser uploads straight to the bucket, so the bucket must
# allow it. Without this the console's upload fails in preflight, which is a
# browser-side refusal the API never sees and nothing in its logs explains:
#
#     [{"AllowedOrigins": ["https://verwaltung.<BASE_DOMAIN>"],
#       "AllowedMethods": ["PUT"],
#       "AllowedHeaders": ["content-type"],
#       "ExposeHeaders":  [],
#       "MaxAgeSeconds":  3000}]
#
# `PUT` only. The console never GETs from the bucket directly — learner media
# is fetched by the browser as a plain navigation with a signed URL, which is
# not a CORS request.
#
# **2. The bucket must not be public.** Every read goes through a presigned URL
# the API mints after the sequence gate agrees, and per-customer isolation is
# the `<customerId>/` key prefix plus the API refusing to sign anything outside
# the caller's own. A public-read bucket makes all of that decoration: the keys
# contain UUIDs and are not guessable, but "not guessable" is not a boundary.
# Verify with an unsigned request — it must be 403:
#
#     curl -sI https://<endpoint>/<bucket>/<any-known-key>
#
# --- Backups (P23-03) ------------------------------------------------------
#
# BACKUP_ENCRYPTION_KEY lives in secrets.env and is generated by deploy.sh.
# **Never regenerate it**: a new value does not re-encrypt anything, it makes
# every existing backup permanently unreadable.
#
# A second bucket, ideally with its own credentials that can write to it and
# cannot delete from the primary. A "backup" in the same bucket as the thing it
# backs up survives a disk failure and not a mistake or a stolen key — and the
# mistake is the failure that actually happens. Empty means backups land in the
# primary bucket under `backups/`, which is better than nothing and is not what
# to run in production.
BACKUP_BUCKET=
BACKUP_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
BACKUP_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# `backup verify` fails when the newest database backup is older than this.
# 26 hours: a daily backup plus two hours of slack, so an overrunning job does
# not page and a missed night does.
BACKUP_MAX_AGE_HOURS=26

# The demo participant's portal password (P25-02).
#
# The seeds create one participant per customer so that
# `fortbildung.digitalspital.com/<slug>` can be signed in to and looked at.
# Leave this **empty** and the seed generates a fresh password from the CSPRNG
# and prints it once — which is the right default, because a password nobody
# chose is a password nobody reuses.
#
# Set it only when re-running the seed against an environment where somebody has
# already written the password down, so the re-run does not invalidate it. It is
# never a production credential: the demo account is meant to be deleted before
# a customer's own physicians use the tenant.
SEED_PARTICIPANT_PASSWORD=

# The MEDICE course's VNR, for the seed only (P30-01).
#
# `packages/seed` stamps this onto `adhs-akademie-adult`. A VNR is the identifier
# of a real accredited event and belongs to MEDICE, so it is **never committed**
# — the seed falls back to nineteen zeros, which is structurally valid, is
# obviously not anybody's, and would be rejected by EIV rather than silently
# reporting a physician against the wrong event.
#
# Set it on an installation that will actually submit. Leaving it unset is safe;
# setting it wrong is not.
SEED_MEDICE_VNR=

# Whether a deploy creates the default customer when the installation has none
# (P26-01). `yes` — the default — or anything else to switch it off.
#
# It is the one seed `deploy.sh` runs by itself, and it runs with `--if-missing`:
# it reads one row and returns before writing anything once `DSCustomer` exists,
# so only an installation that has never had that customer is affected. What it
# creates carries no VNR, no accreditation body and no CME points, so nothing it
# seeds can reach EIV.
#
# Set it to `no` on an installation where the operator wants a genuinely empty
# console — for instance one whose only tenant is a customer's real content and
# where a Lorem-ipsum course in the project picker would be noise.
SEED_DEFAULT_CUSTOMER=yes
